WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.627
What if a robot were told what to do by analogy,
not by an exact goal image?

00:00:05.707 --> 00:00:11.588
This ICML 2022 work from Berkeley and Meta
introduces goal-conditioned bisimulation: an

00:00:11.668 --> 00:00:17.206
abstraction where behaviorally equivalent
state-goal pairs share a representation,

00:00:17.286 --> 00:00:19.330
so goals compose by arithmetic.

00:00:19.710 --> 00:00:25.174
Goal-conditioned reinforcement learning usually
hands the agent the exact goal,

00:00:25.254 --> 00:00:26.437
as a target image.

00:00:26.517 --> 00:00:31.069
But in the real world you rarely know the precise
goal in advance.

00:00:31.149 --> 00:00:36.684
Ask a robot to close a drawer, and you may not know
how the closed drawer looks.

00:00:36.764 --> 00:00:39.210
We want to specify tasks by analogy.

00:00:39.590 --> 00:00:42.388
Consider dicing a carrot versus a radish.

00:00:42.468 --> 00:00:48.075
The objects differ, but the skill and the change
from start to goal are the same.

00:00:48.155 --> 00:00:54.253
Good representations are invariant to irrelevant
details and equivariant to what varies.

00:00:54.333 --> 00:01:00.220
Prior bisimulation handled distractors for one
task; we lift it to families of tasks.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.982
The paper makes three contributions.

00:01:03.062 --> 00:01:05.786
It defines goal-conditioned bisimulation,

00:01:05.866 --> 00:01:09.273
grouping state-goal pairs that behave equivalently.

00:01:09.353 --> 00:01:14.676
It gives a metric-learning objective so new goals
arise from latent arithmetic.

00:01:14.756 --> 00:01:20.010
And it proves the representation is sufficient for
any state-only reward task.

00:01:20.390 --> 00:01:22.668
GCB learns two encoders together.

00:01:22.748 --> 00:01:29.100
Phi encodes a state-goal pair so its L1 distance
matches an on-policy bisimulation metric,

00:01:29.180 --> 00:01:32.173
capturing how differently two tasks behave.

00:01:32.253 --> 00:01:37.818
Psi encodes a state so the goal embedding minus the
state embedding equals phi.

00:01:37.898 --> 00:01:42.463
That makes goals composable: add an analogous pair
to your state,

00:01:42.543 --> 00:01:45.607
then take the nearest neighbor in psi space.

00:01:45.687 --> 00:01:48.680
Training is offline on Implicit Q-Learning.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:56.992
GCB is tested in a PyBullet manipulation suite of
random workspaces built from eighty-four object geometries.

00:01:57.072 --> 00:02:00.667
Tasks span Drawer, Button-and-Drawer, and Analogy,

00:02:00.747 --> 00:02:05.298
each also run with added video distractors to probe
robustness.

00:02:05.378 --> 00:02:07.650
Results average over five seeds.

00:02:08.030 --> 00:02:13.097
Across six settings, GCB is top on five, and wins
on every distractor task.

00:02:13.177 --> 00:02:19.891
It reaches about forty-five percent on Drawer with
distractors and thirty-two on Button-and-Drawer.

00:02:19.971 --> 00:02:24.078
On analogy tasks, where the goal is inferred from
an example,

00:02:24.158 --> 00:02:26.960
it roughly doubles the next-best baseline.

00:02:27.340 --> 00:02:29.531
Ablations confirm the design.

00:02:29.611 --> 00:02:33.526
Adding a grounding term, phi of a goal with itself,

00:02:33.606 --> 00:02:37.521
as a normalizing constant in psi's objective helps.

00:02:37.601 --> 00:02:40.262
An L1 metric loss for phi beats L2.

00:02:40.342 --> 00:02:45.040
And varying the latent dimensionality barely
changes control.

00:02:45.420 --> 00:02:48.692
Some headline numbers.

00:02:48.772 --> 00:02:52.043
On Drawer with video distractors, GCB reaches zero
point four four eight,

00:02:52.123 --> 00:02:55.395
best of any method.

00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:58.747
On Button-and-Drawer with distractors, zero point
three two two.

00:02:58.827 --> 00:03:02.098
On analogy,

00:03:02.178 --> 00:03:05.450
zero point four zero three versus zero point one
seven six for the next best.

00:03:05.830 --> 00:03:11.397
The takeaway: treating bisimulation as an
equivalence over tasks, not just states,

00:03:11.477 --> 00:03:13.876
aligns analogous tasks in one space.

00:03:13.956 --> 00:03:17.044
Because goals become composable by arithmetic,

00:03:17.124 --> 00:03:21.658
you can point a robot at an example and have it
infer its own goal,

00:03:21.738 --> 00:03:25.170
provably sufficient for any state-only reward task.
